The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) itself is not platform-independent, but Java programs running on the JVM are.

💡 Here's the difference:
| Term                  | Platform Independence                                       |
| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Java Code (.java)** | ✅ Yes — platform-independent (write once, run anywhere)     |
| **Bytecode (.class)** | ✅ Yes — same on all platforms                               |
| **JVM**               | ❌ No — platform-dependent (specific to OS and architecture) |


✅ Why Java Is Called Platform-Independent
You write your code once in Java

The Java compiler (javac) converts it into bytecode

This bytecode is interpreted/run by the JVM

Since every OS has its own platform-specific JVM, the same bytecode can run on any machine with a compatible JVM

🔁 Example:
You compile a .java file into .class on Windows

You take that .class file and run it on:

Linux JVM

macOS JVM

Android JVM

It works the same, because each OS has its own JVM implementation

🔍 Conclusion
JVM = platform-dependent

Java (via JVM) = platform-independent

So Java's "Write Once, Run Anywhere" magic comes from its bytecode + JVM combination.